WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.397
Before we focus on agentic rag, we need a broader idea: agentic A I.

00:00:06.397 --> 00:00:09.842
An L L M by itself is not an agent.

00:00:09.842 --> 00:00:16.852
If I send one prompt to a model and receive one answer, that is simply a model call.

00:00:16.852 --> 00:00:24.767
Agentic behavior appears when software around the model keeps track of state, chooses among approved actions,

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:31.028
observes the result of an action, updates what it knows, and decides what should happen next.

00:00:31.575 --> 00:00:33.547
Think about a travel assistant.

00:00:33.547 --> 00:00:39.532
A normal model might answer a question from its training data.

00:00:39.532 --> 00:00:45.897
An agentic system could first check the destination, then search current flight information, then compare options,

00:00:45.897 --> 00:00:48.752
and then stop when it has enough evidence.

00:00:48.752 --> 00:00:53.082
The important part is the controlled sequence of decisions.

00:00:53.082 --> 00:00:56.662
Our learning order is deliberate.

00:00:56.662 --> 00:00:58.582
First, understand the agent loop.

00:00:58.582 --> 00:01:02.567
Then understand tools and state.

00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:05.932
After that, compare classic rag with agentic rag.

00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:10.868
Finally, use Lang Chain and Lang Graph to organize the same ideas.

00:01:11.318 --> 00:01:19.600
By the end, you should be able to explain why a workflow is agentic, what belongs in state, when a tool is appropriate,

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.620
and how to stop a loop safely.
